  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/dianema-longibarbis",
      "html": "<p>Porthole catfish, Dianema longibarbis, is a peaceful callichthyid catfish from the Amazon basin in Peru and Brazil. It is named for the row of spots running along the lateral line. All specimens in the trade are wild-caught and new imports tend to be delicate and susceptible to disease, but once acclimatised it usually proves very hardy and can live for over 10 years.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/dianema-longibarbis",
      "html": "<p>Widespread throughout the Amazon river basin in Peru and Brazil.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/dianema-longibarbis",
      "html": "<p>Mainly found away from the main river channels in slower moving creeks, tributaries, floodplain lakes and ponds.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "aquariumSize",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/dianema-longibarbis",
      "html": "<p>Standard length: 10 cm SL. Aquarium base: 90 × 38 cm. Volume: ~103 litres.</p><p>A 90 × 38 × 30 cm tank (~103 litres) is big enough to house a small group of these.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/dianema-longibarbis",
      "html": "<p>A biotope tank would consist of a sandy substrate with tangles of driftwood and roots. Its habitat could be further simulated by the addition of a few handfuls of dried leaves scattered over the base of the tank. It is equally happy in other types of setup though, provided there is plenty of cover. Heavily-planted tanks are particularly suitable.</p>",
      "fixed": true
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/dianema-longibarbis",
      "html": "<ul><li>Temperature: 24–28°C</li><li>pH: 5.5–7.5</li><li>Hardness: 1–20 dGH</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/dianema-longibarbis",
      "html": "<p>An unfussy feeder. Offer a varied diet consisting of dried sinking pellets and small live and frozen foods such as bloodworm, brine shrimp and chopped earthworms. Once settled, it will often feed from the surface.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/dianema-longibarbis",
      "html": "<p>A very peaceful fish that is suitable for many communities. It will not harm even the smallest of tankmates, although tiny fry may be hoovered up. Ideal tankmates include other Amazonian species such as tetras, peaceful cichlids, Corydoras and smaller Loricariids. It is less suitable for a community of much larger or aggressive species, due to its shy, retiring nature. It is totally unaggressive towards conspecifics. Although it can be kept as a single specimen if you wish, you will find it far more outgoing and active if you buy a small group of 3-6 fish. Oddly for a catfish, you will often see it swimming in a shoal in midwater when maintained in this way.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/dianema-longibarbis",
      "html": "<p>The leading few rays of the pectoral fins are thickened slightly in males. Mature females are likely to be rounder in the body than males.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/dianema-longibarbis",
      "html": "<p>Very scant information exists regarding the spawning of this fish in captivity.</p>",
      "fixed": true
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/dianema-longibarbis",
      "html": "<p>So named because of the row of spots running along the lateral line, the porthole cat is a good community species. Newly imported fish tend to be a little delicate and are susceptible to disease. This is at least in part because all specimens sold for the trade are wild caught. Once acclimatised, it usually proves very hardy and can be exceptionally long-lived for a fish of this size, having the potential to survive for over 10 years.</p>"
    }
  ],
